## [ISSUE] `.small-action-desc` shrinks to its content, pulling the floated edit/delete buttons in from the right edge

**Severity:** MEDIUM — the edit/delete controls on small-action posts (pin, close, split and similar) move from the right edge of the post row to right after the text, and the row's width depends on the text length.

### The Problem

```scss
.small-action {
  @include flexbox();          // line 264 (new)
  @include align-items(center);
  ...
  .small-action-desc { padding: 0 1.5%; ... }   // no flex-grow / width
  button { background: transparent; border: 0; float: right; }
}
```

Template `components/small-action.hbs`: the `<button>`s for delete/edit are children of `.small-action-desc`, so they float right *inside the description box*.

### Theory / Proof

- Before: `.small-action-desc` was a normal block, so it filled the 755px `.small-action` row (`desktop/topic-post.scss:730`). `float: right` put the buttons at the far right of the post.
- After: `.small-action-desc` is a flex item with the defaults `flex: 0 1 auto`. Its main size is its max-content width (CSS Flexbox L1, section 9.2 "flex base size" with `flex-basis: auto` / `width: auto` → content size). It no longer stretches across the free space.
- Floats are placed inside their containing block, which is now only as wide as the text plus the buttons. So the buttons end up right after "closed this topic 3 days ago", not at the row's right edge.

### What happens today vs. what will break

Every staff user sees small-action posts with edit/delete buttons. After this PR the buttons' horizontal position changes from post to post with the description length, instead of lining up in a column on the right.

### Research

The usual flexbox "media object" pattern gives the body `flex: 1` (for example, Bootstrap 4's `.media-body { flex: 1; }`), so the text column takes the remaining space.

### Recommendation

```scss
.small-action-desc {
  @include flex(1);          // or: -webkit-box-flex: 1; -ms-flex: 1; -webkit-flex: 1; flex: 1;
  padding: 0 1.5%;
  ...
}
```

## [ISSUE] Mobile `.custom-message { margin-left: -40px }` now overflows because the padding it offset was removed

**Severity:** LOW — on mobile, custom messages on small-action posts (for example a close/archive reason) spill left over the icon column.

### The Problem

Common rule, changed in this PR:

```scss
.small-action-desc {
  padding: 0 1.5%;          // line 280 — was: padding: 0.5em 0 0.5em 4em;
```

The mobile override the PR leaves untouched (`app/assets/stylesheets/mobile/topic-post.scss:516-522`):

```scss
.small-action .small-action-desc {
  p { padding-top: 0; }
  .custom-message { margin-left: -40px; }
}
```

### Theory / Proof

- Before: the description had a 4em (about 50px at 0.9em of a 14px base) left padding, and the -40px margin moved the custom message back into that padding so it used the full width under the icon. It stayed inside the description's border box.
- After: the left padding is 1.5% of the container width (about 5px on a 320px phone). A -40px margin now puts the message about 35px left of the description's border box, over the `.topic-avatar` icon (`mobile/topic-post.scss:525`, `padding: 0; margin: 0`), since there is no `overflow: hidden` to clip it.

### What happens today vs. what will break

Desktop is fine because it has no negative margin. On mobile, any small-action post with `post.cooked` (the `custom-message` branch in `components/small-action.hbs`) draws its text over the icon.

### Research

The negative margin was a counterweight to the old 4em padding. When the padding changes, the counterweight has to change with it.

### Recommendation

Remove or retune the mobile offset together with the new padding:

```scss
// app/assets/stylesheets/mobile/topic-post.scss
.small-action .small-action-desc {
  p { padding-top: 0; }
  .custom-message { margin-left: 0; }
}
```

## [ISSUE] `align-items` mixin maps values 1:1 onto legacy properties with different keywords

**Severity:** LOW — current callers (`center`, `baseline`) work. The first `flex-start`/`flex-end` call will quietly fail on the legacy engines the mixin exists for.

### The Problem

```scss
@mixin align-items($alignment) {
    -webkit-box-align: $alignment;
    -webkit-align-items: $alignment;
    -ms-flex-align: $alignment;
    -ms-align-items: $alignment;
    align-items:$alignment;
}
```

### Theory / Proof

- `-webkit-box-align` (2009 spec) accepts `start | end | center | baseline | stretch`.
- `-ms-flex-align` (IE10, 2012 tweener spec) accepts `start | end | center | baseline | stretch`.
- Modern `align-items` uses `flex-start | flex-end | center | baseline | stretch`.
- `-ms-align-items` is not a property in any IE/Edge version. IE10 uses `-ms-flex-align`, and IE11 supports unprefixed `align-items`.

So `@include align-items(flex-start)` gives `-webkit-box-align: flex-start` and `-ms-flex-align: flex-start`, which are both invalid and dropped. Old WebKit and IE10 then fall back to `stretch`.

### What happens today vs. what will break

Today's calls (`center` in header.scss and topic-post.scss, `baseline` in badges.css.scss) share the same keyword across all syntaxes, so they work. A new mixin in `foundation/mixins.scss` will be reused, and `flex-start`/`flex-end` are the most common alignment values.

### Research

Bourbon's `align-items` mixin and Compass's flexbox mixins translate `flex-start` to `start` and `flex-end` to `end` for the old-syntax properties for this reason. Autoprefixer does the same.

### Recommendation

```scss
@mixin align-items($alignment) {
  $legacy: $alignment;
  @if $alignment == flex-start { $legacy: start; }
  @else if $alignment == flex-end { $legacy: end; }
  -webkit-box-align: $legacy;
  -ms-flex-align: $legacy;
  -webkit-align-items: $alignment;
  align-items: $alignment;
}
```

## [ISSUE] `order` mixin ignores the 0-based vs 1-based difference between `order` and `box-ordinal-group`

**Severity:** LOW — current uses (`order(2)`, `order(3)`) keep their relative order. `order(0)`/`order(-1)` or `order(1)` against default items break on the old box model.

### The Problem

```scss
@mixin order($val) {
  -webkit-box-ordinal-group: $val;
  -moz-box-ordinal-group: $val;
  -ms-flex-order: $val;
  -webkit-order: $val;
  order: $val;
}
```

### Theory / Proof

- `order` (Flexbox L1): integer, **default 0**, negatives allowed.
- `box-ordinal-group` (2009 spec): **positive** integer, **default 1**. 0 and negative values are invalid and dropped.

Because the default items sit at `order: 0` but `ordinal-group: 1`, the mapping is off by one:
- `order(1)` puts an item after the defaults in the modern syntax, but groups it *with* them in the legacy syntax (1 == default 1), so it stays in DOM order.
- `order(-1)` (the usual way to move something first) is invalid in the legacy syntax and does nothing.

### What happens today vs. what will break

`.extra-info-wrapper` (order 2) and `.panel` (order 3) are both above 1, so their relative order is right in both syntaxes. The mixin is shared, though, and the next caller writing `order(-1)` or `order(1)` gets different layouts in old WebKit/Gecko than in modern browsers.

### Research

Bourbon's legacy `order` mixin and Autoprefixer both emit `-webkit-box-ordinal-group: $val + 1`, so the 2009 syntax stays consistent with the modern one.

### Recommendation

```scss
@mixin order($val) {
  -webkit-box-ordinal-group: $val + 1;
  -moz-box-ordinal-group: $val + 1;
  -ms-flex-order: $val;
  -webkit-order: $val;
  order: $val;
}
```
